[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **complete, working example** that shows how to

1. **Load a PMTiles file** (a single-file container for vector tiles) with the official `pmtiles` library.  
2. **Add it as a vector source** to a MapLibre GL JS map.  
3. **Create a line layer** that draws the features from that source.

The code works with **MapLibre GL JS v6** (the current major release).  
If you are still on v5 you’ll need the old import style, but the rest of the logic is the same.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the required packages

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm i maplibre-gl pmtiles
# If you are using a bundler (Vite, webpack, etc.) you’ll also need the CSS:
npm i maplibre-gl@^6.0.0   # pins the major version
```

> **Note for bundlers** – v6 requires a one-time `setWorkerUrl()` call (see the migration guide).  
> The snippet at the bottom of this answer shows the Vite-specific call; replace it with the appropriate one for your toolchain.

---

## 2️⃣ Minimal HTML (CDN) version

If you just want a quick demo without a build step, you can use the CDN-only approach.  
Because v6 ships **ESM-only**, the script tag must be `type="module"` and you import the ESM build of both libraries.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>PMTiles → MapLibre line layer</title>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
  <!-- MapLibre CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  // ---- 1️⃣ Import the libraries -------------------------------------------------
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
  import { PMTiles } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@latest/dist/index.js';

  // ---- 2️⃣ Register the pmtiles protocol ---------------------------------------
  // This makes URLs that start with "pmtiles://" resolve to the file you give it.
  const pmtiles = new PMTiles('https://YOUR_DOMAIN/path/to/your-file.pmtiles');
  // Register the protocol handler with the global fetch implementation used by MapLibre.
  // (MapLibre uses the native fetch API under the hood.)
  self.fetch = new Proxy(self.fetch, {
    apply(target, thisArg, args) {
      const [input] = args;
      if (typeof input === 'string' && input.startsWith('pmtiles://')) {
        // Strip the protocol and let the PMTiles instance handle it.
        return pmtiles.fetch(input.replace('pmtiles://', ''));
      }
      return Reflect.apply(target, thisArg, args);
    }
  });

  // ---- 3️⃣ Create the map -------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},          // we’ll add our source later
      layers: []            // we’ll add our layer later
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // Wait until the style is ready before adding sources / layers
  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // ---- 4️⃣ Add the PMTiles vector source ------------------------------------
    map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
      type: 'vector',
      // The URL must use the custom protocol we registered above.
      // The part after the protocol is the *path* inside the PMTiles archive.
      // Usually you just use the root (empty string) because the archive
      // contains a single tileset.
      url: 'pmtiles://'
    });

    // ---- 5️⃣ Add a line layer --------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
      // The name of the layer inside the vector tileset (check with `pmtiles info` or
      // look at the source’s metadata). Replace "roads" with whatever your source uses.
      'source-layer': 'roads',
      paint: {
        // Simple styling – a thick blue line with a white halo.
        'line-color': '#0066ff',
        'line-width': 3,
        'line-opacity': 0.9,
        'line-gap-width': 0,
        'line-blur': 0,
        'line-cap': 'round',
        'line-join': 'round',
        // Optional halo to make the line pop on dark basemaps
        'line-outline-color': '#ffffff',
        'line-outline-width': 1
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What the code does**

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Import** | Pulls the ESM builds of MapLibre GL JS and the `pmtiles` helper. |
| **Protocol registration** | Wraps `fetch` so any request that starts with `pmtiles://` is delegated to the `PMTiles` instance. This is the official way the `pmtiles` library integrates with MapLibre. |
| **Map creation** | Starts a blank style (empty `sources`/`layers`). You could also use a full-blown style URL if you prefer. |
| **`style.load`** | Guarantees the style JSON is parsed before we add our own source/layer. |
| **`addSource`** | Declares a vector source that points at the PMTiles archive via the custom protocol. |
| **`addLayer`** | Draws a line from the `source-layer` named `"roads"` (replace with your own layer name). The `paint` object contains a simple line style. |

