Below is a **complete, working example** that shows how to

1. **Load a PMTiles file** (a single-file MBTiles/Vector-Tile archive) with the official `pmtiles` library,  
2. **Add it as a vector source** to a MapLibre GL JS v6 map, and  
3. **Draw a line layer** that uses one of the source’s layers.

The snippet works both **in the browser via a CDN** and **in a bundler-based project** (Vite, webpack, etc.).  
I also point out the v6-specific gotchas you’ll run into if you’re coming from a v5 code base (no default export, ESM-only, `setWorkerUrl()` for bundlers, etc.).

---

## 1️⃣ Install / import the two libraries

| Environment | How to get the code |
|-------------|---------------------|
| **Plain HTML (CDN)** | No npm install needed – just pull the ESM builds from unpkg. |
| **Bundler (npm / yarn / pnpm)** | `npm i maplibre-gl pmtiles` (or `yarn add …`). |

### CDN version (no build step)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>PMTiles + MapLibre GL JS v6</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; left:0; right:0; bottom:0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  // 1️⃣ Import the two ESM bundles
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
  import { PMTiles } from 'https://unpkg.com/pmtiles@^3/dist/index.mjs';

  // 2️⃣ Register the pmtiles:// protocol handler (once per page)
  const pmtiles = new PMTiles('https://example.com/data/my-tiles.pmtiles');
  pmtiles.addProtocol();   // makes “pmtiles://…” URLs work

  // 3️⃣ Create the map (no worker-url call needed for CDN)
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},          // we’ll add our source later
      layers: []            // we’ll add our layer later
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // 4️⃣ When the map is ready, add the vector source that points at the PMTiles file
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
      type: 'vector',
      // The protocol handler turns this into a fetch of the .pmtiles file.
      url: 'pmtiles://my-tiles.pmtiles'
    });

    // 5️⃣ Add a line layer that draws from a source-layer inside the PMTiles.
    //    Replace "roads" with the actual layer name that exists in your tileset.
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
      'source-layer': 'roads',          // <-- change to your source-layer name
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#ff6600',
        'line-width': 2,
        'line-opacity': 0.8
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

> **Why the `pmtiles://` URL?**  
> The `PMTiles` class registers a custom fetch-handler that intercepts any request whose URL starts with `pmtiles://`. Inside the handler it reads the appropriate tile from the single-file archive and returns it as a normal vector-tile response, so MapLibre can treat it exactly like a remote `https://…/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` endpoint.

